            <div xml:id="d422">

                <pb facs="1217" n="1067" sameAs="frus:frus1907p2/pg1067"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 9757.</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to
                        Chargé Brown.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of State</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1907-11-13"><hi rendition="#italic">November 13,
                        1907</hi></date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 287.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I inclose a copy of a letter in which the
                    foreign secretary of the American Board of Commissioners for Foreign Missions
                    requests the department to endeavor to prevent the opening by Turkish officials
                    of the mail of American missionaries.</p>
                <p>You are requested to remind the Sublime Porte of its assurances given in 1892,
                    and the positive orders which it issued in the latter part of April of that year
                    not to detain letters addressed to Americans. (See <ref
                        target="frus:frus1892/pg_561">Foreign Relations, 1892, p. 561</ref>.)</p>
                <p>I am, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">E. Root.</hi></signed></closer>


                <xi:include href="d422-a1.xml" xpointer="d422-a1"/>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
